{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.137,
            0.046,
            0.346,
            0.063
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 8"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.088,
            0.848,
            0.163
          ],
          "content": "If a locker is toggled an odd number of times, it will be open. Otherwise, it will be closed. The number of times a locker is toggled is the same as the number of factors of the locker number. For example, for locker #6, Person 1 opens it, Person 2 closes it, Person 3 opens it and Person 6 closes"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.162,
            0.632,
            0.235
          ],
          "continues_prev": true,
          "content": "it. The numbers 1, 2, 3, and 6 are factors of 6. If the number of factors is even, the locker will be toggled by an even number of people and it will eventually be closed."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.235,
            0.631,
            0.328
          ],
          "content": "Note that each factor of a number has a ‛partner factor’ so that the product of the pair of factors yields the given number. Here, 1 and 6 form a pair of partner factors of 6, and 2 and 3 form another pair."
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "bbox": [
            0.648,
            0.168,
            0.84,
            0.274
          ],
          "sub_type": "code",
          "content": "```txt\n6:\n1 × 6\n2 × 3\nFactors are\n1, 2, 3 and 6.\n```",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.33,
            0.671,
            0.353
          ],
          "content": "Does every number have an even number of factors?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.174,
            0.39,
            0.338,
            0.465
          ],
          "content": "1: 1 × 1 The only factor is 1."
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "bbox": [
            0.411,
            0.373,
            0.591,
            0.477
          ],
          "sub_type": "code",
          "content": "```txt\n4:\n1 × 4\n2 × 2\nFactors are\n1, 2 and 4.\n```",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.659,
            0.374,
            0.788,
            0.475
          ],
          "content": "9: 1 × 9 3 × 3 Factors are 1, 3 and 9."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.502,
            0.847,
            0.54
          ],
          "content": "We see in some cases, like $2 \\times 2 ,$ that the numbers in the pair are the same."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.542,
            0.848,
            0.581
          ],
          "content": "Can you use this insight to find more numbers with an odd number of factors?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.586,
            0.848,
            0.662
          ],
          "content": "For instance, 36 has a factor pair 6 × 6 where both numbers are 6. Does this number have an odd number of factors? If every factor of 36 other than 6 has a different factor as its partner, then we can be sure that 36 has an odd number of factors. Check if this is true."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.671,
            0.79,
            0.691
          ],
          "content": "Hence all the following numbers have an odd number of factors —"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.377,
            0.695,
            0.621,
            0.714
          ],
          "content": "1 \\times 1 , 2 \\times 2 , 3 \\times 3 , 4 \\times 4 , \\ldots",
          "image_source": "images/page_1_equation_13.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.719,
            0.849,
            0.814
          ],
          "content": "A number that can be expressed as the product of a number with itself is called a **square number,** or simply a **square**. The only numbers that have an odd number of factors are the squares, because they each have one factor which, when multiplied by itself, equals the number. Therefore, every locker whose number is a square will remain open."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.091,
            0.915,
            0.112,
            0.937
          ],
          "content": "2"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.448,
            0.97,
            0.551,
            0.985
          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
